{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs",
  "item": "fn[whole_comment]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This callback fills the Profile interface for every non-Rust profile.\n Those profiles all set hashes_comment to never, so Builder::encode skips\n comment_prose for every source input; plain text does not use the grammar\n encoder at all. Rust hashes doc comments through its separate doc_prose.\n The fallback therefore has no reachable behavior in the supported profiles."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs#fn[whole_comment]` is appropriate. It sits on an explicit function with a body, and its fenced commentary gives a substantive reason: the fallback is unreachable in the supported profiles. The current source agrees with each claim in that reason.\n\n- **Callers:** The `comment_prose` field of `Profile` is only called from `Builder::encode` in `outline/mod.rs`. That call comes after `(self.profile.hashes_comment)(node, self.source)` returns true.\n- **Non-Rust profiles:** The css, markdown, python, toml, typescript, tsx and yaml profiles all set `hashes_comment: never` and `comment_prose: ...whole_comment`. `never` always returns false, so `comment_prose` is never called for them.\n- **Plain text:** The plain-text profile in `outline/plain.rs` has `language: None` and also uses `never` and `whole_comment`.\n- **Rust:** The Rust profile uses `hashes_comment: is_doc_comment` and `comment_prose: doc_prose`, so it never reaches `whole_comment`.\n- **Custom profiles:** The custom and test profiles copy fields from the Rust profile, so they don't change this.\n\nThe commentary limits its claim to the supported profiles, so a public custom provider is not a contradiction.",
